@noindent
Such a sequence of pairs, formed by nested @code{cons}es, is called a
@newterm{list}, and this edition provides it as the generic type
@code{List<T>}, so a helper constructor builds the chain in one
expression.@footnote{In this book, we use @newterm{list} to mean a
chain of pairs terminated by the end-of-list marker.  In contrast, the term
@newterm{list structure} refers to any data structure made out of pairs, not
just to lists.}  The above sequence could be produced by
@code{List::from_iter([1, 2, 3, 4])}.  In general,

@example rust
let list = List::from_iter([a1, a2, @dots{}, an]);
@end example

@noindent
is equivalent to

@example rust
let list = List::cons(
    a1,
    &List::cons(a2, &List::cons(@r{@dots{}}, &List::cons(an, &List::Nil))),
);
@end example

@noindent
Lisp systems conventionally print lists by printing the sequence of elements,
enclosed in parentheses, and this edition's @code{List} displays itself the
same way.  Thus, the data object in @ref{Figure 2.4} is displayed as
@code{(1 2 3 4)}:

@example rust
let one_through_four: List<i128> = List::from_iter([1, 2, 3, 4]);

println!("@{one_through_four@}");
// => (1 2 3 4)
@end example

@noindent
Be careful not to confuse the expression @code{List::from_iter([1, 2, 3, 4])}
with the list @code{(1 2 3 4)}, which is the value obtained when the
expression is evaluated.  Scheme blurs the distinction because an unevaluated
expression can itself be taken as data; Rust draws the line in the type
system, and the list of @ref{Figure 2.4} is a value of type
@code{List<i128>}, never a piece of program.

We can think of @code{car} as selecting the first item in the list, and of
@code{cdr} as selecting the sublist consisting of all but the first item.
Nested applications of @code{car} and @code{cdr} can be used to extract the
second, third, and subsequent items in the list.@footnote{Since nested
applications of @code{car} and @code{cdr} are cumbersome to write, Lisp
dialects provide abbreviations for them---for instance,
@code{(cadr ⟨@var{arg}⟩)} is @code{(car (cdr ⟨@var{arg}⟩))}.  Rust provides no
such abbreviations, and neither does this edition: a chain of
@code{car}/@code{cdr} steps that can fail is spelled out step by step, with
@code{?} carrying the failure outward, as exercise 2.25 shows.} The
constructor @code{cons} makes a list like the original one, but with an
additional item at the beginning.

@example rust
println!("@{@}", one_through_four.car().copied().expect("cons cell"));
// => 1

println!("@{@}", one_through_four.cdr().expect("cons cell"));
// => (2 3 4)

println!(
    "@{@}",
    one_through_four
        .cdr()
        .and_then(List::car)
        .copied()
        .expect("cons cell"),
);
// => 2

println!("@{@}", List::cons(10, &one_through_four));
// => (10 1 2 3 4)

println!("@{@}", List::cons(5, &one_through_four));
// => (5 1 2 3 4)
@end example

@noindent
The value of @code{List::Nil}, used to terminate the chain of pairs, can be
thought of as a sequence of no elements, the @newterm{empty list}.  The word
@newterm{nil} is a contraction of the Latin word @emph{nihil}, which means
``nothing.''@footnote{It's remarkable how much energy in the standardization
of Lisp dialects has been dissipated in arguments that are literally over
nothing: Should @code{nil} be an ordinary name?  Should the value of
@code{nil} be a symbol?  Should it be a list?  Should it be a pair?  This
edition sidesteps the entire issue the way the languages born after the
brawls do: the empty list is a variant, @code{List::Nil}, and the type system
knows which values are lists and which are not.  Chapter @ref{2.3} introduces
the symbolic data that motivates Scheme's own notation for the empty list;
this edition's lists stay constructed values throughout.}

@subsubheading List operations

The use of pairs to represent sequences of elements as lists is accompanied by
conventional programming techniques for manipulating lists by successively
``@code{cdr}ing down'' the lists.  For example, the procedure @code{list-ref}
takes as arguments a list and a number @math{n} and returns the @math{n^{\hbox{th}}} item of
the list.  It is customary to number the elements of the list beginning with 0.
The method for computing @code{list-ref} is the following:

@itemize @bullet

@item
For @math{{n = 0}}, @code{list-ref} should return the @code{car} of the list.

@item
Otherwise, @code{list-ref} should return  the @math{{(n - 1)}}-st item of the
@code{cdr} of the list.

@end itemize

@example rust
pub fn list_ref<T: Clone>(
    items: &List<T>,
    n: usize,
) -> Result<T, SchemeError> @{
    match (items, n) @{
        (List::Cons(x, _), 0) => Ok(x.clone()),
        (List::Cons(_, rest), _) => list_ref(rest, n - 1),
        (List::Nil, _) => @{
            Err(SchemeError::TypeMismatch("list-ref: index past the end".into()))
        @}
    @}
@}

let squares: List<i128> = List::from_iter([1, 4, 9, 16, 25]);

println!("@{@}", list_ref(&squares, 3)?);
// => 16
@end example

@noindent
Often we @code{cdr} down the whole list.  To aid in this, the book's Scheme
primitive @code{null?} tests whether its argument is the empty list; this
edition spells the test as the @code{is_empty} method on @code{List}, which
the type system admits only for lists in the first place.  The procedure
@code{length}, which returns the number of items in a list, illustrates this
typical pattern of use:

@example rust
pub fn length<T>(items: &List<T>) -> usize @{
    match items @{
        List::Nil => 0,
        List::Cons(_, rest) => 1 + length(rest),
    @}
@}

let odds: List<i128> = List::from_iter([1, 3, 5, 7]);

println!("@{@}", length(&odds));
// => 4
@end example

@noindent
The @code{length} procedure implements a simple recursive plan. The reduction
step is:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The @code{length} of any list is 1 plus the @code{length} of the @code{cdr} of
the list.

@end itemize

@noindent
This is applied successively until we reach the base case:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The @code{length} of the empty list is 0.

@end itemize

@noindent
We could also compute @code{length} in an iterative style, which Rust writes
as the loop it is:

@example rust
pub fn length_iter<T>(items: &List<T>) -> usize @{
    let mut count = 0;
    let mut cursor = Some(items);
    while let Some(List::Cons(_, rest)) = cursor @{
        count += 1;
        cursor = Some(rest);
    @}
    count
@}
@end example

@noindent
Another conventional programming technique is to ``@code{cons} up'' an answer
list while @code{cdr}ing down a list, as in the procedure @code{append}, which
takes two lists as arguments and combines their elements to make a new list:

@example rust
println!("@{@}", append(&squares, &odds));
// => (1 4 9 16 25 1 3 5 7)

println!("@{@}", append(&odds, &squares));
// => (1 3 5 7 1 4 9 16 25)
@end example

@noindent
@code{Append} is also implemented using a recursive plan.  To @code{append}
lists @code{list1} and @code{list2}, do the following:

@itemize @bullet

@item
If @code{list1} is the empty list, then the result is just @code{list2}.

@item
Otherwise, @code{append} the @code{cdr} of @code{list1} and @code{list2}, and
@code{cons} the @code{car} of @code{list1} onto the result:

@end itemize

@example rust
pub fn append<T: Clone>(list1: &List<T>, list2: &List<T>) -> List<T> @{
    match list1 @{
        List::Nil => list2.clone(),
        List::Cons(x, rest) => List::cons(x.clone(), &append(rest, list2)),
    @}
@}
@end example

