@noindent
In building up a complex image in this manner, we are exploiting the fact that
painters are closed under the language's means of combination.  The
@code{beside} or @code{below} of two painters is itself a painter; therefore, we
can use it as an element in making more complex painters.  As with building up
list structure using @code{cons}, the closure of our data under the means of
combination is essential to the ability to create complex structures while using
only a few operations.  Once we can combine painters, we would like to be able
to abstract typical patterns of combining painters.  We will implement the
painter operations as Rust procedures.  This means that we don't need any
special abstraction mechanism in the picture language.  Since the means of
combination are ordinary procedures, we automatically have the capability to do
anything with painter operations that we can do with procedures.  For example,
we can abstract the pattern in @code{wave4} as

@example rust
fn flipped_pairs_procedure(painter: &Painter) -> Painter @{
    let painter2 = beside(painter, &flip_vert(painter));
    below(&painter2, &painter2)
@}
@end example

@noindent
and define @code{wave4} as an instance of this pattern:

@example rust
let wave4 = flipped_pairs_procedure(&wave);
@end example

@subsubheading Recursive painting operations

We can also define recursive operations.  Here's one that makes painters split
and branch towards the right as shown in @ref{Figure 2.13} and
@ref{Figure 2.14}:

@example rust
pub fn right_split(painter: &Painter, n: u32) -> Painter @{
    if n == 0 @{
        return Rc::clone(painter);
    @}
    let smaller = right_split(painter, n - 1);
    beside(painter, &below(&smaller, &smaller))
@}
@end example

